== Long Cases

=== 1. Chronic Cholecystitis & Choledocholithiasis
- *Q: What are the presentations and signs of chronic cholecystitis and choledocholithiasis?*
  *A:* Pain is in the epigastric or right hypochondriac region and may radiate to the tip of the right shoulder or back. Murphy's sign is positive in acute cholecystitis. Radiation is to the back, while referred pain is to the tip of the right shoulder due to irritation of the diaphragm (supplied by the phrenic nerve C3, C4). Charcot's triad for cholangitis/choledocholithiasis consists of intermittent jaundice, abdominal pain, and fever with chills and rigor.

- *Q: How do you differentiate this clinically from PUD, Chronic Pancreatitis, or Gastric Ca?*
  *A:* (Note: Answer not found in source texts) Differentiate clinically by the timing of pain (PUD relates to meals, pancreatitis radiates straight through to the back). Changes in bowel habit are seen in gastric/colon malignancies.

- *Q: What investigations are necessary?*
  *A:* USG of the whole abdomen pays special attention to the hepatobiliary system, looking for a distended gallbladder and acoustic shadows. (Note: Answer not found in source texts) Upper GI Endoscopy is used to rule out PUD and biopsy periampullary lesions. ERCP/Cholangiogram can identify and remove common bile duct stones.

- *Q: What are the management and surgical details for these conditions?*
  *A:* Medical management for an appendicular/gallbladder lump includes the Ochsner-Sherren regimen (NPO, NG suction, IV fluids, IV antibiotics, IV analgesics). Laparoscopic Cholecystectomy advantages include being minimally invasive, having small incisions, less pain, early ambulation, and less hospital stay. Structures injured during Lap Chole include the right hepatic artery and recurrent laryngeal nerve (general lap risk). (Note: Answer not found in source texts) Calot's triangle is bounded by the cystic duct, common hepatic duct, and the inferior border of the liver. Incisions include the Upper Midline or Right Subcostal (Kocher's incision). Layers cut in midline: skin, subcutaneous tissue, linea alba, fascia transversalis, parietal peritoneum. Head rising test checks for incisional hernias. Choledocholithotomy may utilize a T-tube. The T-tube rests the sutured bile duct and prevents bile leakage. Vitamin K is used to prevent perioperative bleeding due to lack of bile salt-mediated fat-soluble vitamin absorption. Remove T-tube on the 11th POD if the cholangiogram is clear.

=== 2. Chronic Pancreatitis
- *Q: What is the presentation and what are the concepts associated with Chronic Pancreatitis?*
  *A:* Persistent dull aching pain with periodicity. Pain is relieved by the Mohammedan prayer sign (leaning forward). Associated with history of alcohol and smoking. Ascites mechanism leads to shifting dullness. Fibrocalculous Pancreatic Diabetes (FCPD) involves tail involvement causing diabetes mellitus. It is termed "acute on chronic" because there are acute exacerbations of inflammation over a chronically fibrosed and calcified pancreas.

- *Q: What are the investigations and management strategies?*
  *A:* USG shows a swollen, edematous pancreas with peripancreatic fluid. Confirmatory tests include CT scan which can detect calcifications and pseudocysts. (Note: Answer not found in source texts) Medical management focuses on pancreatic enzyme replacement, analgesia, and diabetes control. Surgical indications include intractable pain, pseudocysts, and suspicion of malignancy. Stones in specific areas dictate procedures like Puestow (longitudinal pancreaticojejunostomy) or distal pancreatectomy.

=== 3. Gastric Outlet Obstruction (GOO) due to Pyloric Stenosis
- *Q: What are the examination findings and metabolic changes in GOO?*
  *A:* Succussion splash is positive 4-6 hours after a meal. (Note: Answer not found in source texts) Auscultopercussion tests the borders of a dilated stomach. Paradoxical aciduria occurs because the body initially loses HCl, causing the kidneys to excrete HCO3 (alkaline urine), but severe dehydration triggers ADH and aldosterone to retain Na+ and water at the expense of H+ and K+, making the urine acidic again. Electrolyte imbalances include hyponatremia, hypochloremia, and hypokalemia. Corrected by Normal Saline infusion (0.9% NaCl) and IV Potassium supplement.

- *Q: What is the management and what are the complications of GOO surgery?*
  *A:* Stomach preparation utilizes a 3-day gastric lavage with a nasogastric tube and 400-500 ml normal saline to remove residual food, reduce stomach size, and prevent anastomotic leakage. Surgical options include Bilateral Truncal Vagotomy with Gastrojejunostomy (posterior retrocolic isoperistaltic short loop). (Note: Answer not found in source texts) Dumping syndrome (early and late) can occur post-operatively due to rapid transit of hyperosmolar chyme into the jejunum.

=== 4. Ca Rectum / GI Malignancies
- *Q: How does Ca Rectum present and how does it metastasize?*
  *A:* Features include per-rectal bleeding, mucous discharge, tenesmus/morning weeping, and alteration of bowel habits. Cough/hemoptysis indicates lung metastasis. Distant metastasis is blood-borne to the liver, lung, bone, and brain. Krukenberg tumor is a transcoelomic/intracavitary metastasis to the ovary (commonly from Ca Stomach, primarily in fertile premenopausal women).

- *Q: What are the standard investigations for GI malignancies?*
  *A:* Proctoscopy visualizes up to 12 cm, Rigid Sigmoidoscopy up to 25 cm, Flexible Sigmoidoscopy up to 60 cm, and Colonoscopy allows visualization up to 160 cm from the anal verge. Tumor markers include Serum CEA level. Staging investigations involve CXR, USG of abdomen, LFTs, MRI of pelvis, and CT scan.

